course: dictionary
lesson: 241
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Almond is one of them. Ask: what is the sentence about? A nut you eat, or a tree that grows in a field?
explain: «Every spring ... carried on lorries to pollinate California's almond farms» is about trees. Farms are places where crops grow, so almond here means the tree that the nut grows on. The clue is in the words around it.
explain: Context can also show why a word matters. «Some children have a nut allergy, so almonds may be banned in school lunches.» The word banned tells you this is about safety, and the words nut allergy tell you why.
fact: An almond is an oval nut with a brown skin.
fact: Almonds can be eaten raw, roasted, or used in baking, such as almond cake.
fact: The almond tree has pink or white blossom.
fact: «Bees and other insects pollinate many crops, such as apples, strawberries and almonds.»
fact: Some children have a nut allergy, so almonds may be banned in school lunches.
exercise: «Bees and other insects pollinate many crops, such as apples, strawberries and almonds, so without them we would have much less fruit.» What does this tell us about almonds? | A type of bee | *A crop that grows on trees and needs bees to pollinate it | A rule in school | A sweet drink only
exercise: «Every spring ... carried on lorries to pollinate California's almond farms.» What are almond farms? | A cake shop by the sea | Farms that make almond milk only | *Farms that grow almond trees | A farm that keeps cows for milk
exercise: Mia's school says almonds are banned from lunches. Why might that be? | Almonds make lunches too cold | Almonds are too expensive for the school | Almonds are only for bees | *Some children have a nut allergy
test: Ella eats a toasted almond with her apple. What is an almond? | A type of bee | A kind of apple | A pink flower | *An oval nut with a brown skin
test: «The almond tree was covered in pink blossom in March.» What does almond tree mean here? | *The tree the nut grows on | The nut, eaten raw | A bee that pollinates crops | The cake made with almonds
exam: «Some children have a nut allergy, so almonds may be banned in school lunches.» What is the best answer to: why might almonds be banned? | *Because some children are allergic to nuts | Because almonds are too sweet for lunch | Because almonds are too big to eat | Because almonds only grow in California
